.TH MATLOCK 1 "10 July 2026" "tvsos" "TVSOS Commands Manual"

\"------------------------------------------------------------------------------

.SH NAME

.B matlock
\- screen lock for X and Wayland with raining characters like in the film
\fIThe Matrix\fP (1999).

\"------------------------------------------------------------------------------

.SH SYNOPSIS

.SY
.nf
\fBmatlock\fR [\-v] [\-b [<frames>]] [<command> [<args...>]]
.fi
.YS

\"------------------------------------------------------------------------------

.SH DESCRIPTION

.PP
\fBmatlock\fP, or Matrix Lock, is a screen lock program for X and Wayland
written in C++. Initially a fork of the original C implementation
\fBslock\fP, it has undergone considerable restructuring and revamping.

If provided, [<command> [<args...>]] is executed after the screen has been
unlocked.

.SS BACKENDS
.PP
The display backend is chosen at runtime: if \fIWAYLAND_DISPLAY\fP is set,
\fBmatlock\fP locks the session through the Wayland
\fIext-session-lock-v1\fP protocol; otherwise, if \fIDISPLAY\fP is set, the
X11 backend is used. An error is printed when neither is set.

On Wayland, the compositor must support \fIext-session-lock-v1\fP. This
includes niri, sway, river, Hyprland, KWin and other wlroots-based
compositors; GNOME (Mutter) does not implement the protocol and is not
supported. Every output is covered by a lock surface, including outputs
plugged in while the session is locked.

.SS SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
.PP
On X11, to make sure a locked screen can not be bypassed by switching VTs or
killing the X server with Ctrl+Alt+Backspace, it is recommended to disable
them both in \fBxorg.conf(5)\fP for maximum security:

.EX
Section "ServerFlags"
    Option "DontVTSwitch" "True"
    Option "DontZap"      "True"
EndSection
.EE

On Wayland, input exclusivity and the hiding of screen content are
guaranteed by the compositor as part of the session-lock protocol; no
further configuration is needed.

.SS CUSTOMISATION
.PP
\fBmatlock\fP reads \fI/etc/matlock.yaml\fP and then per-user overrides from
\fI$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/matlock/matlock.yaml\fP (usually
\fI~/.config/matlock/matlock.yaml\fP). Both files are watched while the
screen is locked and changes apply live. Only a flat "key: value" YAML
subset is supported.

.TP
.B background
background colour ("#RRGGBB").
.TP
.B font_pattern
\fBfontconfig\fP(1) pattern for the rain glyphs. Both backends rasterise
this font with FreeType and present an identical software-rendered frame.
.TP
.B font_size
pixel size of the closest rain layer (10..128). The farthest layer is fixed
at 10 and the layers between interpolate linearly.
.TP
.B depth_levels
number of depth layers (1..8, default 4). Closer layers are larger,
brighter, faster and longer; the classic four-level look is interpolated
across the configured count.
.TP
.B max_droplets
size of the droplet pool (16..20000, default 1000).
.TP
.B droplet_length
longest stream, in characters (2..200, default 50).
.TP
.B spawn_attempts
droplet spawn attempts per simulation step, each with a 1-in-5 chance
(1..32, default 1). The main rain-density control; raise it for heavier
rain.
.TP
.B fps
animation frame rate (10..120, default 20). The simulation is rescaled so
the on-screen droplet velocity, spawn density and mutation rate are
identical at any value; a lower rate uses less CPU with coarser motion. On
HiDPI Wayland outputs, \fBhidpi\fP below reduces CPU far more than lowering
this.
.TP
.B idle_timeout
seconds without input before the animation freezes and the process sleeps at
near-zero CPU until input resumes it (0..86400, default 0, which disables the
idle pause). When enabled, a key press resumes it on both backends and a
pointer move resumes it on X11. Off by default so the rain never appears to
freeze; enable it to save power while away.
.TP
.B hidpi
render at the output's full buffer scale on HiDPI displays (true/false,
default true). When false, buffers are rendered at scale 1 and the
compositor upscales, cutting CPU and memory on Retina-class outputs at some
crispness cost (Wayland only).
.TP
.B charset
the characters the streams are made of (1..1024 of them, written as UTF-8;
blank and control characters are ignored). Any script may be used: for each
character the font matched by \fBfont_pattern\fP does not contain,
\fBfontconfig\fP(1) is asked for a fallback font that does, so
\fIcharset: कखगघङचछजझञ\fP rains Devanagari even when \fBfont_pattern\fP is a
Latin-only monospaced font. Characters no installed font can draw are
reported on standard error and fall as blanks. The cell of the rain grid is
the widest character in the set, so a script whose characters vary in width,
or one that mixes narrow and wide characters, spaces the columns more loosely
than a monospaced Latin set does. Combining marks (Devanagari matras, Arabic
diacritics, and the like) are not characters that stand on their own and will
draw on a dotted circle: use base letters only.
.TP
.B mutate_chars
whether characters in the falling columns mutate (true/false).
.TP
.B failonclear
treat cleared input as a failed attempt (true/false).
.TP
.B fontcolour
mapping with \fBinit\fP, \fBinput\fP and \fBfailed\fP colours ("#RRGGBB")
for the three states.

.PP
The user and group privileges are dropped to remain compile-time settings in
\fIinclude/config.hpp\fP.

\"------------------------------------------------------------------------------

.SH OPTIONS

.IP "\-v"
print version information to the standard output and exit.

.IP "\-b [<frames>]"
run a headless render benchmark: build the renderer and one simulation from
the loaded configuration, render <frames> frames (default 1000) into an
off-screen buffer, and print the mean milliseconds per frame and peak
resident set size. No display server connection is made and no privileges
are required. The buffer size defaults to 1920x1080 and can be overridden
with the \fIMATLOCK_BENCH_SIZE\fP environment variable (for example
\fI3840x2160\fP).

\"------------------------------------------------------------------------------

.SH FILES

.TP
.I /etc/matlock.yaml
system-wide configuration.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/matlock/matlock.yaml
per-user overrides (usually \fI~/.config/matlock/matlock.yaml\fP).

